.TH RS-TOOLKIT 1 "May 2026" "RS-Toolkit 1.0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rs-toolkit \- Professional Linux System Administration Toolkit
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rs-inspect
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B rs-diagnose
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B rs-fix-*
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B RS-Toolkit
is a comprehensive suite of system administration tools designed to inspect, diagnose, and fix common Linux server issues. Perfect for beginners and professionals alike.

.SH COMMANDS

.SS Inspection Commands (Read-Only)
These commands are safe to run anytime. They only read system information.

.TP
.B rs-inspect
Master health dashboard showing overall system status
.br
\fIExample:\fR rs-inspect

.TP
.B rs-inspect-cpu
View CPU usage, load average, and top processes
.br
\fIExample:\fR rs-inspect-cpu

.TP
.B rs-inspect-mem
Check memory usage, swap, and top memory consumers
.br
\fIExample:\fR rs-inspect-mem

.TP
.B rs-inspect-disk
Analyze disk usage, I/O stats, and space usage
.br
\fIExample:\fR rs-inspect-disk

.TP
.B rs-inspect-network
View network connections, bandwidth, and connectivity
.br
\fIExample:\fR rs-inspect-network

.TP
.B rs-inspect-svc
Check service status and identify failed services
.br
\fIExample:\fR rs-inspect-svc

.TP
.B rs-inspect-security
Security audit including failed logins and firewall status
.br
\fIExample:\fR rs-inspect-security

.TP
.B rs-inspect-logs
Analyze system logs for errors and warnings
.br
\fIExample:\fR rs-inspect-logs

.SS Diagnosis Commands (Analysis)
These commands analyze your system and provide recommendations.

.TP
.B rs-diagnose
Auto-detect all issues with detailed recommendations
.br
\fIExample:\fR rs-diagnose

.TP
.B rs-diagnose-slow
Identify performance bottlenecks (CPU, memory, I/O)
.br
\fIExample:\fR rs-diagnose-slow

.TP
.B rs-diagnose-memory
Memory leak detection and analysis
.br
\fIExample:\fR rs-diagnose-memory

.TP
.B rs-diagnose-disk
Disk space analysis with cleanup recommendations
.br
\fIExample:\fR rs-diagnose-disk

.TP
.B rs-diagnose-network
Network problem diagnosis and troubleshooting
.br
\fIExample:\fR rs-diagnose-network

.SS Fix Commands (Requires Root)
These commands make changes to your system. Always review before running.

.TP
.B rs-fix-disk
Clean disk space by removing old logs, temp files, and cache
.br
\fIExample:\fR sudo rs-fix-disk
.br
\fIDry run:\fR sudo rs-fix-disk --dry-run

.TP
.B rs-fix-memory
Clear system cache and free up memory
.br
\fIExample:\fR sudo rs-fix-memory

.TP
.B rs-fix-services
Restart failed systemd services
.br
\fIExample:\fR sudo rs-fix-services

.TP
.B rs-fix-logs
Rotate and compress old log files
.br
\fIExample:\fR sudo rs-fix-logs

.SS Utility Commands

.TP
.B rs-watch
Continuous monitoring with auto-refresh
.br
\fIExample:\fR rs-watch 5
.br
(Refreshes every 5 seconds)

.TP
.B rs-report
Generate comprehensive system report
.br
\fIExample:\fR rs-report > system-report.txt

.TP
.B rs-help
Show detailed help and usage guide
.br
\fIExample:\fR rs-help

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Display help information for the command

.TP
.B \-\-dry-run
Preview changes without making them (fix commands only)

.SH EXAMPLES

.SS Beginner Examples

.TP
\fBCheck if your server is healthy:\fR
.br
rs-inspect

.TP
\fBFind out why server is slow:\fR
.br
rs-diagnose-slow

.TP
\fBClean up disk space (preview first):\fR
.br
sudo rs-fix-disk --dry-run
.br
sudo rs-fix-disk

.TP
\fBMonitor system in real-time:\fR
.br
rs-watch 5

.SS Advanced Examples

.TP
\fBComplete system checkup:\fR
.br
rs-inspect && rs-diagnose

.TP
\fBGenerate report for support:\fR
.br
rs-report > /tmp/system-report-$(date +%Y%m%d).txt

.TP
\fBFix all common issues:\fR
.br
sudo rs-fix-disk && sudo rs-fix-memory && sudo rs-fix-services

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/rs-*
Command executables

.TP
.I /usr/local/lib/rs-toolkit/common.sh
Shared library functions

.TP
.I /etc/rs-toolkit/config.conf
Configuration file

.TP
.I /var/log/rs-toolkit/audit.log
Audit log of all actions

.SH ENVIRONMENT
No special environment variables required.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success

.TP
.B 1
Error occurred

.TP
.B 2
Warning (non-critical)

.SH SECURITY
.TP
\fBInspection commands\fR can be run by any user
.br
\fBFix commands\fR require root privileges (sudo)
.br
All actions are logged to audit.log

.SH TIPS FOR BEGINNERS

.TP
\fB1. Start with inspection\fR
Always run inspection commands first to understand your system:
.br
rs-inspect

.TP
\fB2. Use diagnosis before fixing\fR
Diagnose issues before attempting fixes:
.br
rs-diagnose

.TP
\fB3. Preview changes\fR
Use --dry-run to see what will be changed:
.br
sudo rs-fix-disk --dry-run

.TP
\fB4. Read the output\fR
Commands use colors and symbols:
.br
✓ Green = Good
.br
⚠ Yellow = Warning
.br
✗ Red = Problem

.TP
\fB5. Get help anytime\fR
Use rs-help or --help flag:
.br
rs-help
.br
rs-inspect --help

.SH TROUBLESHOOTING

.TP
\fBCommand not found\fR
Run: sudo bash install.sh

.TP
\fBPermission denied\fR
Use sudo for fix commands:
.br
sudo rs-fix-disk

.TP
\fBColors not showing\fR
Your terminal may not support colors. Output still works.

